pamsignal (0.4.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Feature: webhook_auth_header config key for the custom webhook
    channel (#7). Operators can send a single arbitrary HTTP header
    (Bearer token, X-API-Key, Splunk HEC, Datadog DD-API-KEY, HMAC,
    etc.) with each webhook POST. Previously the only authenticated
    path was a reverse proxy in front of the receiver injecting the
    header — the bundled examples/nodejs-webhook/ reference receiver
    explicitly required this setup. The header value is rendered into
    the existing memfd-backed curl -K config, so the secret never
    appears in argv or /proc/<pid>/cmdline. Validator rejects CRLF /
    quote / backslash injection at config-load time; setting the
    header without webhook_url is also rejected.
  * Feature: optional mTLS client authentication for the custom
    webhook channel (#8). Three new config keys —
    webhook_client_cert, webhook_client_key, webhook_ca_bundle —
    target the operator profile that already runs PKI (internal CA,
    cert-manager, SPIFFE, service-mesh issuance). Combines additively
    with webhook_auth_header — the corporate-SIEM / Wazuh pattern
    (mTLS for service identity, Bearer for per-request authorization).
    Cert/key/CA paths flow through the same memfd-backed curl config
    as the auth header, so argv stays byte-identical regardless of
    which auth modes are configured. Validator at config load:
    O_NOFOLLOW open (symlinks rejected), regular-file required,
    ownership outside {root, daemon-uid} rejected,
    webhook_client_key additionally rejected if group/world-readable.
    Cross-field consistency: cert without key (or vice versa) is a
    config-load error, and any TLS key without webhook_url is
    rejected. Path strings constrained to printable ASCII minus
    quote and backslash so they are safe in the curl -K quoted
    value.
  * Internal API change in src/notify.c: post_alert() and
    build_secrets_memfd() swap from positional arguments to a
    curl_config_t struct that carries the optional auth header and
    TLS paths by name. The four legacy callers (Telegram, Slack,
    Teams, Discord, WhatsApp) pass struct-literal arguments and
    produce identical wire output.
  * Documentation: docs/configuration.md gains a "Custom webhook
    authentication" section (Bearer + mTLS reference); docs/alerts.md
    extends the "Authentication (optional)" subsection with an
    "Mutual TLS (optional, advanced)" block; docs/architecture.md
    documents the curl_config_t / memfd-backed -K dispatch model;
    docs/deployment.md documents cert/key file placement;
    docs/threat-model.md updates attack #3 (alert credentials
    exposure via process metadata) and rewrites NS8 (HMAC-signed
    payloads remain out of scope; Bearer + mTLS now ship);
    examples/nodejs-webhook/README.md adds an mTLS-enabled
    Express receiver variant.
  * Tests: 20 new CMocka cases in tests/test_config.c covering
    parse + validation paths for both webhook_auth_header (9) and
    mTLS (11). Suite grew 33 -> 53. ASAN + UBSAN clean; clang-tidy
    clean.

 -- Tuan Nguyen <anhtuank7c@hotmail.com>  Fri, 08 May 2026 04:50:00 +0000

pamsignal (0.3.4-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Security: extend the _EXE allowlist to accept sshd-session.
    OpenSSH 9.8 (released 2024-07) split sshd into a privilege-
    separated listener (sshd) and a per-connection auth process
    (sshd-session). On Ubuntu 26.04, Fedora 41+, and Debian
    Trixie, all PAM auth events on sshd are recorded with
    _EXE=/usr/{sbin,lib}/openssh/sshd-session and were dropped
    by pamsignal's anti-spoofing allowlist — the daemon ran fine,
    journald accumulated events fine, the daemon just didn't
    see them. Fix is a one-line addition to the basename
    allowlist. Refactored into a testable ps_is_trusted_exe
    helper with eight new CMocka cases covering canonical paths,
    sshd-session paths, alternate system prefixes, and
    spoofing-rejection paths.
  * Tooling: tests/scenario.sh full-stack local end-to-end
    scenario test (build, install, real sshd brute-force, real
    sudo brute-force, mock HTTPS webhook ingestion, SIGHUP
    reload, purge cleanup). Twelve phases with pass/fail per
    phase and an EXIT trap that always cleans up. Adds two
    pieces of coverage CI doesn't have: explicit Type=notify
    activation verification, and the v0.3.0 sudo brute-force
    scenario with testpamuser added to /etc/sudoers.d/. The
    script caught the sshd-session bug above on its first
    non-CI run.
  * CI: forward-port a hardened Python webhook handler into
    release-packages.yml's test-deb mock. The default
    BaseHTTPRequestHandler does dirty TLS shutdowns (no
    close_notify) which OpenSSL 3.5+ flags as
    error:0A000126:SSL routines::unexpected eof while reading.
    The runner is currently ubuntu-24.04 (OpenSSL 3.0,
    tolerant) but will eventually move to 26.04 (3.5+, strict);
    the fix also adds a READY-poll startup check so a
    Python-side bind error fails loudly with captured stderr.
  * Repository hygiene: CONTRIBUTING.md, GitHub issue templates
    (bug + feature + config) rewritten as Linux-daemon reports
    rather than generic open-source forms, pull-request
    template — these accumulated under "Unreleased" since
    v0.3.3 and ship now alongside the security fix.

